<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Waitangi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Te Tiriti o Waitangi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Waitangi was signed on 6 February 1840 at Waitangi in the Bay of Islands by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-hobson">William Hobson</a> for the British Crown and more than 40 Māori chiefs, later joined by about 500 more. Its English and Māori texts differ on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and governorship, a source of lasting dispute. It led to British annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, and since 1975 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waitangi-tribunal">Waitangi Tribunal</a> has investigated breaches, leading to settlements with iwi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi.jpg" alt="Treaty of Waitangi" width="320" height="599" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Hobson, James Freeman, and James Busby (English version); Henry Williams and Edward Williams (Māori translation) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treatyofwaitangi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
